# macOS

Pontia supports macOS 11 and later on Intel and Apple Silicon. The local control plane and Codex daemon use per-user LaunchAgents. `pontia-edge` is not supported on macOS.

## Install requirements

The release installer requires `curl`, `grep`, `install`, `jq`, `mktemp`, `mv`, `openssl`, `rm`, `shasum`, `tar`, `tr`, `uname`, and `wc`.

The system `openssl` may not provide the required Ed25519 command. The installer automatically checks Homebrew's standard `openssl@3` paths:

```text
/opt/homebrew/opt/openssl@3/bin/openssl
/usr/local/opt/openssl@3/bin/openssl
```

You can point the installer at another compatible executable with `PONTIA_OPENSSL`.

The default binary directory is `$HOME/.local/bin`. Add it to your shell `PATH` before running `pontia init`.

## LaunchAgents

Initialization installs the Pontia service definition at:

```text
~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.pontia.pontiad.plist
```

Manage it with:

```sh
pontia up
pontia status
pontia down
```

For direct diagnosis:

```sh
launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/dev.pontia.pontiad
```

When Codex is selected, Pontia also installs:

```text
~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.pontia.codex-daemon.plist
```

Inspect it with:

```sh
launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/dev.pontia.codex-daemon
```

The LaunchAgent definitions store absolute executable paths and a controlled executable search path. Run `pontia init` again after moving pi, Codex, or their home directories.

## Updates

`pontia update` uses `/usr/sbin/lsof` to verify that the running CLI is the installed file before replacement. Keep `pontia` and `pontiad` together as regular files and run the installed `pontia` directly.

Do not use `sudo` for the local initializer or lifecycle commands. Pontia is installed for the current user and uses that user's home, Agent Client credentials, and GUI LaunchAgent domain.
